---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const features = [
  {
    title: "Admin console",
    body: "Add or remove users, see which phones are enrolled, revoke a lost phone in one click.",
  },
  {
    title: "Lost-phone recovery",
    body: "Pair a new phone and revoke the old one. No support ticket required.",
  },
  {
    title: "Audit log",
    body: "See who signed in, when, and from where, for each app and server.",
  },
  {
    title: "Per-team access lists",
    body: "Control which apps and servers each person can use their phone to sign in to.",
  },
  {
    title: "Bulk onboarding",
    body: "Invite teammates and pair their phones at scale.",
  },
  {
    title: "Managed relay",
    body: "We run the encrypted relay so your team does not have to.",
  },
];
---

<Base title="How it works | Gatebridge">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose-page">
      <h1>How Gatebridge works</h1>
      <p class="lead text-muted">
        Gatebridge turns your Android phone into a hardware-backed security key
        for a remote Linux server. It sits between the server and your phone,
        carrying sign-in requests across the gap.
      </p>

      <h2>What happens when you sign in</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          You visit a website or open an admin panel on your Linux server.
          It asks you to sign in with a security key.
        </li>
        <li>
          A small program on the server (the daemon) receives that request.
        </li>
        <li>
          The daemon forwards the request to your phone over an encrypted
          connection. Your phone never talks to the website directly.
        </li>
        <li>
          Your phone asks for a fingerprint or face. It signs the request
          using a secret that only exists inside the phone's security chip.
        </li>
        <li>
          The signed response goes back to the server. The website sees a
          normal security-key sign-in. It never learns the secret.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What you need</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>An Android phone with fingerprint or face unlock.</li>
        <li>A Linux server you can run a small program on (VPS, bastion, dev box).</li>
        <li>Network access between the two (through our relay, or your own).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What Gatebridge is for</h2>
      <p>
        If your team signs in to remote Linux servers and you want stronger
        protection than passwords, Gatebridge uses the phone everyone already
        carries. No extra hardware to order, ship, or replace.
      </p>

      <h2>What it is not</h2>
      <p>
        Gatebridge does one thing: it lets your phone act as a security key for
        remote servers. It is not a password manager, not a replacement for
        SSH keys, and not a full identity platform. If you need those, other
        tools do them better.
      </p>

      <div class="cta-row mt-12">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" data-waitlist>Get started</button>
        <a href="/security" class="btn btn-secondary">Security model</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose-page {
    max-width: 68ch;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
  }

  .prose-page h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-12);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .prose-page ol,
  .prose-page ul {
    padding-left: var(--space-6);
  }

  .prose-page li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }
</style>
